    {
      "id": "226",
      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 226,
        "body_ja": "",
        "body": "<p>The <span data-term='接続助詞・せつぞくじょし'>conjunction particle</span> <span data-gp-id='416'>て</span>, or the <span data-term='格助詞・かくじょし'>case marking particle</span> <span data-gp-id='36'>で</span> are often used to highlight when something is being 'used'. The thing being used does not necessarily need to indicate a tool, but can also indicate a circumstance/situation. In these cases, '(A) <span data-gp-id='36'>で</span> (B)' translates as 'with/by means of (A), (B)'.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180594'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180595'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180596'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180597'>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<section class='funfact'><h4>Fun Fact</h4>\r\n<p>With <span data-gp-id='416'>て</span> (and <span data-gp-id='36'>で</span>), the nuance is quite often that '(A) allowed/led to (B)'. This may be a simple sequence of events, or may be through the direct use of a tool/situation. The nuance that is being highlighted will always depend on the (B) phrase. Due to this, it is important to always read the (B) clause, before deciding what the speaker is trying to convey.</p></section>"
      }
    },
